@schema x.code/v1 { version: 1, relations: [x.code/touches, x.code/exercises] }

@decision d/g8a8e503bbff0-1 { status: cited, source: { kind: file, ref: docs/releasing.md@8a8e503bbff0 }, "code:kind": act, "ingest:quote": "**Order matters.** crates.io resolves path dependencies by version, so a crate cannot be published before everything it depends on." }
~ Publishing order matters due to path dependencies.

Crates.io resolves path dependencies by version, so a crate cannot be published before everything it depends on.

@decision d/g8a8e503bbff0-2 { status: cited, source: { kind: file, ref: docs/releasing.md@8a8e503bbff0 }, "code:kind": act, "ingest:quote": "Publishing 0.2.0 stopped after the fifth with:\n\n```\n429 Too Many Requests: You have published too many new crates in a short period of time.\n```" }
~ Rate limit handling is implemented with retries and checks.

The rate limit refills from the last success, not from the first refusal, so the last three crates took about forty minutes and the final one seven attempts.

@decision d/g8a8e503bbff0-3 { status: cited, source: { kind: file, ref: docs/releasing.md@8a8e503bbff0 }, "code:kind": act, "ingest:quote": "**Test the publish, not the pipe.** `cargo publish … | tail -3` in a retry loop tests `tail`, which always succeeds; that reported two crates published when both had been refused." }
~ Testing cargo's exit status instead of piping into `tail`.

A retry loop must test cargo's exit status: piping into `tail` tests the pipe, which is how two crates were reported published while both had been refused.

@decision d/g8a8e503bbff0-4 { status: cited, source: { kind: file, ref: docs/releasing.md@8a8e503bbff0 }, "code:kind": decline, "ingest:quote": Eight crates publish; `cargo-smysl-eval` does not (`publish = false` — it is the evaluation tooling and reads files outside its own package). }
~ `cargo-smysl-eval` is excluded from publishing.

`cargo-smysl-eval` does not (`publish = false` — it is the evaluation tooling and reads files outside its own package).

@decision d/g8a8e503bbff0-5 { status: cited, source: { kind: file, ref: docs/releasing.md@8a8e503bbff0 }, "code:kind": act, "ingest:quote": "- **Its own `README.md`.** A published package cannot reach outside itself, so each crate has a short one and the full documentation stays in the repository." }
~ Each crate has its own `README.md`.

A published package cannot reach outside itself, so each crate has a short one and the full documentation stays in the repository.

@decision d/g8a8e503bbff0-6 { status: cited, source: { kind: file, ref: docs/releasing.md@8a8e503bbff0 }, "code:kind": act, "ingest:quote": "- **`rust-version = \"1.86\"`**, the MSRV CI checks for the shipped crates." }
~ `rust-version = "1.86"` is set for all crates.

The workspace version is `0.2.0-dev` on the development branch and `0.1.0` at the tag. Publishing 0.1.0 means publishing from `main` at that tag, where the manifests say `0.1.0`. A pre-release version such as `0.2.0-dev` can be published, but `cargo install cargo-smysl` will not pick it up without `--version 0.2.0-dev`, so it is rarely what anyone wants.

@decision d/g8a8e503bbff0-7 { status: cited, source: { kind: file, ref: docs/releasing.md@8a8e503bbff0 }, "code:kind": act, "ingest:quote": "- **Keywords and categories** from the workspace, except `cargo-smysl` itself, which carries its own: it is the crate people install and they find it by searching for a cargo subcommand." }
~ Keywords and categories are set from the workspace, except for `cargo-smysl` itself.

Keywords and categories from the workspace, except `cargo-smysl` itself, which carries its own: it is the crate people install and they find it by searching for a cargo subcommand.

@decision d/g8a8e503bbff0-8 { status: cited, source: { kind: file, ref: docs/releasing.md@8a8e503bbff0 }, "code:kind": act, "ingest:quote": "**Tag and merge before or immediately after publishing, not later.** For a few hours 0.2.0 was on crates.io while the repository's only tag was `v0.1.0`, so `cargo install` and a browser gave different answers about what this project was." }
~ Tagging and merging are done before or immediately after publishing.

For a few hours 0.2.0 was on crates.io while the repository's only tag was `v0.1.0`, so `cargo install` and a browser gave different answers about what this project was.

@decision d/g8a8e503bbff0-9 { status: cited, source: { kind: file, ref: docs/releasing.md@8a8e503bbff0 }, "code:kind": act, "ingest:quote": The version in the manifest is the version that publishes. }
~ The version in the manifest is the version that publishes.

Publish from the branch whose manifests carry the version you mean, and tag that same commit. A pre-release such as `0.2.0-dev` can be published, but `cargo install cargo-smysl` will not pick it up without `--version 0.2.0-dev`, so it is rarely what anyone wants.

@decision d/g8a8e503bbff0-10 { status: cited, source: { kind: file, ref: docs/releasing.md@8a8e503bbff0 }, "code:kind": act, "ingest:quote": "**`cargo package` fails before the first publish, and that is expected.** Until `cargo-smysl-git` exists on crates.io, any crate depending on it reports `no matching package named cargo-smysl-git found`. Only wave 1 can be packaged or verified locally; the rest can be checked only as the waves land." }
~ `cargo package` fails before the first publish, and this is expected.

`cargo package` fails before the first publish, and that is expected. Until `cargo-smysl-git` exists on crates.io, any crate depending on it reports `no matching package named cargo-smysl-git found`. Only wave 1 can be packaged or verified locally; the rest can be checked only as the waves land.

@decision d/g8a8e503bbff0-11 { status: cited, source: { kind: file, ref: docs/releasing.md@8a8e503bbff0 }, "code:kind": act, "ingest:quote": "Publishing to crates.io is a separate, later step, and it is the only irreversible one: a version can be yanked but never removed." }
~ Publishing is a separate step from tagging and merging.

Publishing to crates.io is a separate, later step, and it is the only irreversible one: a version can be yanked but never removed.

@decision d/g8a8e503bbff0-12 { status: cited, source: { kind: file, ref: docs/releasing.md@8a8e503bbff0 }, "code:kind": act, "ingest:quote": "Then publish only what is missing, in wave order, ten minutes apart." }
~ Checking what landed before resuming publishing.

Nothing is lost when that happens — the crates already accepted stay accepted, and the rest publish when the clock allows. Check what landed before resuming:

